-
-\textbf{Architecture.} Detection is two stages on the device.
-\emph{Stage 1}, inside the messaging application, reads message $m_t$
-with the $k$ preceding messages, each prefixed by its sender, and emits
-a probability over the 14 triggers and, for a demand, its sub-type; we
-compared DistilBERT and ModernBERT at 512 and 8,192 tokens across
-$k \in \{0,\ldots,15\}$, and a rank-64 LoRA adapter over a frozen
-Gemma-4-E4B, the class of model phones already ship, which reaches
-$0.986 \pm 0.005$ conversation SCAM-F$_1$ where prompting the same model
-fails at 0.06 macro-F$_1$. \emph{Stage 2}, a unidirectional GRU over the
-$[T \times 14]$ trigger sequence, re-scores the conversation after every
-message with a terminal head for the scam decision and an auxiliary
-head for whether setup has begun; it is unidirectional by requirement,
-since a detector that runs as messages arrive cannot condition on the
-future. Only trigger vectors are buffered; message text is never stored
-or transmitted. The pipeline runs in Element X for Android through
-ONNX Runtime Mobile at 435\,ms per message; export is lossless, dynamic
-INT8 quantization is the entire on-device accuracy gap (recall 0.804 to
-0.686), so FP16 is the deployment artifact.
-
-\textbf{Evaluation protocol.} Model selection uses nested 5-fold
-cross-validation over pooled training data with the test partition
-untouched~\cite{cawley2010overfitting,varma2006bias}; every reported
-number is a mean over 3 seeds with bootstrap 95\% intervals; results
-are reported per source as well as pooled, and entire sources and
-families are held out to test transfer. Detection lead time relative to
-the first validated demand is the deployment-critical metric, and every
-deployment claim is backed by bulk replay of the held-out partition
-through the Android client on real hardware.
-
-\textbf{Results to date.} On the evidence side, real scam conversations
-show a median of 2 public-guidance indicators before the first demand
-(51.8\% show 2 or more), while benign conversations with a demand show
-a median of 0 and reach the demand with none in 90.1\% of cases;
-relationship-building indicators rise steeply with conversation length
-(claims of affection 11\% to 82\% across length quartiles) while the
-investment offer falls. On the model side, the nested-cross-validation
-selection reaches 0.863 $\pm$ 0.005 conversation SCAM-F$_1$ over 3
-seeds on a 1,297-conversation held-out partition under the labels it was
-trained on; under the revised rule-derived labels the same models score
-0.70, because the partition gains 22 positives they never saw, which is
-why retraining under the rule is the first remaining task.
-
-\subsection{Remaining Tasks for SIRENBERT}
-\label{sec:tasks}
-
-\textbf{RT1 --- Re-annotation under the revised guide and retraining.}
-Version 7 of the annotation guide records structured evidence
-citations, drops the intent field, and quarantines invalid output;
-after a 200-conversation pilot with human review, the full corpus is
-re-annotated and the encoders and recurrent model retrained under the
-rule-derived labels, with nested cross-validation, 3 seeds, and
-bootstrap intervals as before.
-
-\textbf{RT2 --- Generalisation and robustness.} Leave-one-family-out
-evaluation (train on romance and investment, test on advance fee and
-employment, and the converse) tests whether the trigger stream
-transfers across families; the adversarial suite (distractor padding,
-paraphrase, slow-play stretching of the lifecycle) quantifies robustness
-bounds. A facet ablation on two triggers decides whether finer
-distinctions add detection value.
-
-\textbf{RT3 --- Cross-application state and user-facing warnings.}
-Platform migration is a scripted step, so the exploitation often
-completes in a different app from the grooming; the on-device design
-will carry trajectory state across applications under explicit consent,
-and an IRB-approved study will evaluate warning timing and framing with
-users who are already emotionally invested.
+SIRENBERT investigates whether observable behaviors, interpreted in sequence,
+can support early warnings about conversational scams on a mobile device.
+A text-classification and recurrent-model prototype has been implemented,
+including an Android messaging-client integration. Establishing the reliability
+of its supervision, its ability to generalize, and the usefulness of its warnings
+remains part of this dissertation.
+
+\subsection{Problem, scope, and working representation}
+A \emph{conversational scam} is a multi-turn exchange in which a suspect uses
+false pretenses to induce a target to part with money or other economic value
+through a request or instruction. The three defining aspects are a multi-turn
+interaction, deception, and a financial demand. The study includes romance,
+investment, advance-fee, employment, and related financial scams where these
+aspects are present. Our evaluated scope focuses on suspect-requested financial
+actions; coercion-based sextortion is held separately.
+
+This problem definition is broader than what a transcript can establish.
+We do not independently verify an occupation, emergency, or promised return.
+For annotation, we identify observable behavior and assess whether an explicit
+financial demand is supported as exploitative by its preceding context.
+A demand provides a reproducible reference point for studying a scam attempt;
+no completed payment is required, and the demand need not end the transcript.
+Earlier concerning behavior can warrant an early-warning hypothesis without
+being labeled a confirmed scam attempt. A direct request for money, by itself,
+is insufficient to establish exploitation.
+
+The current vocabulary contains fourteen triggers (Table~\ref{tab:triggers}).
+They describe conversational functions rather than scam families or proven
+intent. For example, wealth and professional standing can both supply a claim
+to credibility, motivating their initial grouping. We will test that grouping
+through annotation reliability and finer-granularity comparisons. A common
+state transition is not sufficient proof that distinctions are unnecessary,
+and assigning every message a label does not establish complete behavioral
+coverage. Independent review will identify missed known acts, overlaps hidden
+by single-label precedence, and relevant acts absent from the vocabulary.
+
+\begin{table}[tb]
+\centering\small
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.24\linewidth}Xp{.14\linewidth}@{}}
+\toprule
+Trigger & Observable behavior & State floor \\
+\midrule
+WrongNumber\_Intro & Mistaken-contact framing with visible continued engagement & Contact \\
+Introduction\_Opener & Initial greeting or introduction & Contact \\
+SmallTalk\_Maintenance & Routine discussion, logistics, or acknowledgment & Contact \\
+Trust\_Building & Personal disclosure or invitation to confide & Relationship \\
+Affectionate\_Language & Warmth without explicit romantic commitment & Relationship \\
+Credential\_Positioning & Standing, resources, or expertise presented for credibility & Setup \\
+Gain\_Offer & Prospective financial benefit offered to the target & Setup \\
+Verification\_Avoidance & Repeated checking deflection or suspect-controlled proof & Setup \\
+Platform\_Migration & Proposal to continue on another communication channel & Setup \\
+Love\_Bombing & Explicit romantic declaration or commitment & Setup \\
+Crisis\_Disclosure & Acute personal emergency presented & Setup \\
+Emotional\_Manipulation & Guilt, obligation, or relational leverage & Setup \\
+Urgency\_Pressure & Pressure for prompt action & Setup \\
+Financial\_Demand & Request/instruction that would cost the target economic value & Guarded \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\caption{Working behavioral vocabulary. These acts also occur in legitimate
+exchanges. State floors govern bookkeeping, not the legitimacy of a message.
+The legacy name Love\_Bombing denotes the operational declaration test here.}
+\label{tab:triggers}
+\end{table}
+
+Let $a_t$ denote the trigger for suspect message $t$, and let the ordered state
+$s_t\in\{0,1,2,3,4\}$ represent no observed behavior, initial contact,
+relationship building, pre-exploitation behavior, and a demand after setup.
+Define $b(a)$ as 1 for contact/maintenance, 2 for relationship behaviors, and 3
+for the eight setup behaviors. The current deterministic summary is
+\begin{equation}
+ s_0=0,\qquad
+ s_t=\begin{cases}
+ 4, & a_t=D\ \text{and}\ s_{t-1}\geq3,\\
+ \max(s_{t-1},1), & a_t=D\ \text{and}\ s_{t-1}<3,\\
+ \max(s_{t-1},b(a_t)), & a_t\neq D,
+ \end{cases}
+ \label{eq:state}
+\end{equation}
+where $D$ denotes Financial\_Demand. Thus ordinary messages do not erase
+previously observed setup, and setup need not be preceded by a rapport phase
+(Figure~\ref{fig:lifecycle}). This is a working summary of observed behavior,
+not a claim that every person follows a monotonic psychological process.
+
+\begin{figure}[tb]
+\centering\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{\input{figures/lifecycle_graph}}
+\caption{Illustrative transitions in the working lifecycle summary. All states
+can persist, and observed conversations may skip stages. Reaching the final
+state does not itself establish exploitation.}
+\label{fig:lifecycle}
+\end{figure}
+
+Conversation labels are derived separately. Let $q_t=1$ only when a demand is
+interpreted as exploitative and its cited earlier setup messages support that
+interpretation. Citations must resolve to the correct preceding messages;
+human review must assess their semantic support. For an eligible, reviewed
+conversation $C$, the operational rule is
+\begin{equation}
+Y(C)=\begin{cases}
+\textsc{scam}, & \exists t:\ a_t=D\ \land\ q_t=1,\\
+\textsc{suspicious}, & \text{otherwise, if a setup trigger occurs},\\
+\textsc{non-scam}, & \text{otherwise}.
+\end{cases}
+\label{eq:label}
+\end{equation}
+Unresolved annotations will be withheld from conversation-level supervision
+rather than converted into negative targets. The state and label can disagree:
+a legitimate payment request after a crisis can reach state 4 without satisfying
+Equation~\ref{eq:label}'s scam condition.
+
+\subsection{Proxy dataset and preliminary observations}
+Direct, complete records of private scam conversations are scarce. We therefore
+assembled a proxy corpus combining recorded exchanges, public screenshots,
+ordinary dialogue, and legitimate persuasive requests. LoveFraud02 contains
+researcher-recorded exchanges with purported romance-fraud perpetrators;
+Reddit Scambait provides screenshot-based exchanges involving people who knowingly
+engage suspected scammers~\cite{faber2024lovefraud02,lu2026readthis}.
+Neither source should be described as a representative sample of unsuspecting
+victims. Missing screenshots, selective posting, performative responses, and
+uncertain role attribution limit what they establish.
+
+Topical-Chat and a DailyDialog-derived subset provide ordinary dialogue, while
+PersuasionForGood provides financially consequential but legitimate donation
+requests~\cite{gopalakrishnan2019topical,li2017dailydialog,wang2019persuasion}.
+The latter is particularly important for distinguishing persuasion from fraud.
+Generated investment/relationship conversations and report-grounded ScamForge
+conversations extend the available behavior combinations. Their intended scam
+or benign status is a generation condition, not a verified label. They will
+support controlled stress tests and augmentation experiments, with primary
+real-source evaluation reported separately.
+
